---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import lab from "../data/lab.json";
---
<Layout
  title="How we count — Crawl Policy Index"
  description="Plain-language explanation of what the Crawl Policy Index measures, and what it does not."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">How we count</p>
    <h1>What these numbers mean — and what they don’t.</h1>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      This index records what websites <strong>say</strong> about AI crawlers in a public
      file called robots.txt. It is not a measure of traffic, and it is not a scrape of
      page content.
    </p>

    <h2>In one minute</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>We look at a fixed list of 1,000 websites (a test list, not “the web”).</li>
      <li>Once a day we fetch each site’s public crawl rules.</li>
      <li>We record whether the site named a bot such as GPTBot or ClaudeBot, and whether it allowed, blocked, or mixed that bot.</li>
      <li>If a site never named the bot, we do not pretend it made a decision about that bot.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is robots.txt?</h2>
    <p>
      It is a short public file most websites already publish. Search engines have used it
      for years. It is the site speaking in public: “this automated visitor may look around,
      that one may not.” Anyone can read it. We do.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do <strong>not</strong> open articles, product pages, or comment threads. We also
      do not yet measure paywalls or HTTP “pay to crawl” responses. Those are a different
      kind of rule and are out of scope here.
    </p>

    <h2>Named vs a blanket “no”</h2>
    <p>
      Many sites have one rule that says “block everyone.” That is a real policy, but it is
      not a decision about GPTBot or ClaudeBot in particular. Headlines on this site count
      <strong>named</strong> rules: the site wrote that bot’s name.
    </p>
    <p>
      A site with no readable rules file is left out of the count. We do not treat silence
      as permission.
    </p>

    <h2>Why you should not write “the web”</h2>
    <p>
      These figures come from a test list of 1,000 well-known sites, snapshot
      {lab.generated_at.slice(0, 10)}. The bot names have not finished a human
      double-check against each company’s own documentation. Treat this as a lab preview,
      not a published finding, and not a percentage of the internet.
    </p>

    <h2>Training, search, and a person asking</h2>
    <p>Companies often run more than one bot. We keep them apart on purpose:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Training AI</strong> — collecting pages to build a model (GPTBot, ClaudeBot).</li>
      <li><strong>AI search</strong> — collecting pages to answer questions or cite sources.</li>
      <li><strong>When a person asks</strong> — fetching one page because a human requested that link.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A publisher can block training and still allow search. Mixing those two into “AI bots”
      hides the story.
    </p>

    <h2>Switches that are not crawlers</h2>
    <p>
      Names such as Google-Extended and Applebot-Extended are opt-out signals. They do not
      crawl. Never add them into a “how many crawlers were blocked” total.
    </p>

    <h2>Googlebot is a sanity check</h2>
    <p>
      Almost no serious site blocks Google’s main search crawler. If our tables ever showed
      a large share doing that, the bug would be in our reading, not in the web.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping the record honest</h2>
    <p>
      We store the original file so we can re-read it if our software is wrong. A mistake
      is fixed by reading the same files again — not by quietly changing yesterday’s number.
      Every figure on the site should be explainable from this page. If it is not, do not cite it.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
